ng that you find  pleasant it might be useful to actually  know what people do in fact desire and  enjoy even if it's not the sort of thing  that you consider to be good or healthy  or noble so he talks quite a bit about a  number of different kinds of pleasures  you know some are perfectly fine going  out on a spring day and enjoying the air  bending down to pick some hyacinths or  crocuses or lilies or whatever it  happens to be enjoying the sound of rain  falling on the windows above all of  those you know cuddling up with a kitten  or a puppy  you know caressing a loved one all of  those are quite good right but those  aren't the full range of things that we  actually do enjoy Dostoevsky says and  here it's interesting he's got this  strong contrast running throughout the  entire thing between the ordinary person  of action and the intellectual but here  he all the way there are some pleasures  that he attributes to the intellectual  he doesn't say that the ordinary people  don't also indulge in some of these so  these are the non wholesome pleasures  one of these is causing other people to  suffer he mentions how Cleopatra is  reputed to have shoved these pins golden  pins you know so a luxury item through  the breasts of her slaves just to cause  them pleasure no it's cause them pain to  cause herself pleasure and he says you  know people do that sort of thing out of  boredom and you might think of all the  different sort of sadistic actions that  people from children all the way up to  the age have done in order to make  themselves feel alive for a moment by  inflicting some suffering on somebody  else usually somebody who they have  control over somebody who they can pay  somebody who they can compel in  when somebody who has to take it that is  a kind of pleasure he also talks about  the intellectual who is groaning with a  toothache there's this incredibly funny  passage in here where he's talking about  how after the third or fourth day  they're not groaning the same way as say  a peasant was would be he says here we  go the moans that they're feeling  expressed a whole bunch of things he  says listen I'm the second or third day  of pain when he's no longer moaning the  way he did on the first day simply  because his toothache  he's been affected by education and by  European civilization he moans like a  man who as they say nowadays has been  uprooted from the soil and lost contact  with the people his moans soon becomes  strident and perverse and they continued  day and night he certainly knows he's  not helping himself by moaning like that  no one knows better than he that he's  tormenting and and irritating himself  and others for nothing that his audience  and this includes his family for whom  he's trying so hard is listening to him  with disgust  they don't believe he's sincere in the  least and realized he could moan  differently more simply with all these  trills and flourishes and that he's  putting it all on out of sheer spite and  viciousness now a little bit after that  he says there's voluptuous pleasure and  all this degradation and the realization  of it but some of that pleasure comes  from imposing that on everybody around  you the groans the you know endless  suffering that there you're making them  deal with why not just close the door  and or tell them to go on vacation or  something like that and another instance  of this would be the underground man's  insistence with this officer that he you  know have his saber down the right way  and he finally imposes his will on that  that impertinent officer who keeps  coming to him he's a petty  bureaucrat who's acting like a jerk to  this this military officer because he  can and so that's one type of pleasure  causing other people suffering or pain  another thing that that Dostoevsky has  the underground man talk about is doing  things that are bad knowing that they're  bad things that cannot be undone things  that are based things that are as we  would say today nasty or disgusting and  he talks about this and he says to begin  with he says I was conscious of things  that are very wholesome the beautiful  and the good right  and he says IIIi knew about these things  and then I did ugly things things that  everyone does probably but I did  precisely at moments when I was most  aware that they shouldn't be done  he doesn't say what these are but we  could well imagine and he says the more  conscious I was of the good and the  beautiful  the deeper I sank into the mud and the  more likely I was to remain mired in it  now did he simply have a negative  reaction saying oh I'm such a bad guy  this really sucks I feel pain because I  look at myself and how degraded I am no  he took pleasure in it  and I think many people in fact do it  Dostoevsky is onto something quite  important here he says first I went  through this inner struggle I was  ashamed I you know tried to stop doing  these things and then I started to reach  a point where I felt a secret unhealthy  base little pleasure in to creeping back  into my hole after some disgusting night  in Petersburg and forcing myself to  think I'd again done something filthy  that what I had done couldn't be undone  and I inwardly know out at myself for it  tore at myself ate myself away so he's  feeling remorse he's feeling these  negative emotions and then he says then  something happened the bitterness  transformed into a shameful accurse in  sweetness and finally into a great  unquestionable  yes yes definitely a pleasure I mean it  and he says that's why I started out on  the subject I wanted to find out whether  others experienced this kind of pleasure  too and then he says I'll explain it to  you I derived pleasure precisely from  the blinding realization of my  degradation I realized that there was  something wrong with me and at first I  felt bad about that and I still do feel  bad about it but precisely in the  feeling bad I get to feel good there's a  compensation a pleasure voluptuousness  within that so doing bad things becoming  bad becoming a person who does these bad  things these base things over and over  again the degradation as he talks about  it that can produce a pleasure in quite  a few people they they delight in being  called the degenerate gambler or they  you know find a way to turn their  addiction into something cool or you  know we might go on and on and on  another thing he talks about as  providing a certain kind of perverse  pleasure on the part of the intellectual  in this case is despair at the  hopelessness of the situation that he  finds himself in if he can't really be  anything he can't actually deal with  life the way that ordinary people do  everything that he looks at every path  in front of him it makes sense but it  also you know in a way has all sorts of  holes in it and doesn't make sense in  certain ways and he can't really commit  to anything everywhere he looks there's  some sort of problem that can make you  feel hopeless that can cause you to feel  despair and Dostoevsky  through his character tells us well  there's a certain pleasure even in that  perhaps this is the pleasure that leads  us when we are in a losing situation and  there's nothing that we can do about it  that leads us to laugh some people  that is a kind of hysteria if we're  thinking in terms of the underground man  an alternative explanation is possible  that it is either an expression of or it  is a means to taking some sort of  pleasure in the face of hopelessness  finally he also talks about suffering  itself and he talks about the toothache  and and he says you know I can enjoy it  good to think you know and people are  like how can you actually do that well  he says there is pleasure in a toothache  once I suffered a toothache for a whole  month I can tell you there's pleasure in  it people don't rage in silence they  moaned they're not ordinary moans  they're malicious and in this moaning  you express the pleasure of the sufferer  if you didn't enjoy it you wouldn't moan  you would silently suffer it the moaning  itself could be an expression of a kind  of pleasure now I think a lot of people  would hear that and say you're nuts  I don't moan or a groan or gasp or  whatever it happens to be because I'm  feeling pleasure I feel pain but with  time and for some people it can become  its own kind of pleasure and there are  people by the way who do this with just  the physical pain itself there there are  cases of people who and we're not just  talking about masochism in the sense of  enjoying being humiliated by somebody  else or even having pain inflicted upon  oneself but they have something going on  with their body and this is why we often  talk about this as morbid they begin to  feel a kind of pain in it by the way  this is not a realization unique to  Dostoevsky somebody else who talked  about this back in the ancient world  Epicurus believe it or not although it's  it's just one passage then the last  thing that he talks about is this this  cycle this dynamic of what he calls  humiliation  leading to punishment and then feeling a  sense of remorse and repentance but  knowing that he doesn't really feel it  and he's not going to follow through on  it and he's not going to become a better  person in the the end he talks about  this here we go  he says that how can one have the  slightest respect for a man who tries to  find pleasure in the feeling of  humiliation itself I'm not saying that  out of any market sense of repentance in  general I couldn't stand saying sorry  papa I'll never do it again  but it wasn't because I wasn't capable  of saying it on the contrary I was too  prone to say it and you should have seen  under what circumstances - I'd get  myself blamed almost purposely for  something with which I had nothing to do  even in thought or dream that was the  most disgusting but even so I was always  deeply moved I repented my wickedness  and I cried in this of course I was  deceiving myself though I never did so  deliberately and then he says if you ask  me now why tortured and tormented myself  like that I'll tell you I was bored just  sitting with my arms folded so I went in  for all those tricks believe me it's  true just watch yourself carefully  you'll understand that's the way it  works I made up whole stories about  myself and put myself through all sorts  of adventures to satisfy at any price my  need to live how many times did I  convince myself I was offended just like  that for no reason at all and I knew I  had nothing to be offended about I law I  was strongly tempted to play tricks of  this sort that in the end I lost all  restraint he puts himself into these  imaginary dynamics as if life isn't  already hard enough and doesn't give us  enough trouble he gets himself into  trouble but it allows him to take a  certain secret delight so if we actually  want to understand human nature if we  want to deal with human beings as they  are and understand ourselves we need to  recognize that people do in fact take  pleasure in all sorts of things that are  quite unwholesome quite unhealthy bad  base but they do that and that is  perhaps part of the nature of this crazy  complex thing that we call humanity  you
 

